```text
✅✅ Nginx + Node.js Concurrency Model ✅✅

======================== Why Nginx Exists In Front Of Node.js ========================

Node.js can directly serve requests:

Client
  ↓
Node.js

But in production:

Client
  ↓
Nginx
  ↓
Node.js

Nginx provides:

SSL termination
load balancing
rate limiting
static file serving
connection buffering

Node focuses on:

business logic
database access
API handling

======================== Event Loop In Nginx ========================

Each Nginx worker process runs:

Single Thread
Single Event Loop

Uses OS facilities:

epoll (Linux)
kqueue (BSD/macOS)

Nginx is event-driven.

Worker does not create one thread per request.

Example:

1000 clients connected

Worker Process
    ↓
Event Loop
    ↓
Tracks all sockets

Most connections are:

waiting
idle
keep-alive

CPU usage remains low.

======================== Event Loop In Node.js ========================

Node.js also uses:

Single Thread
Single Event Loop

Implemented through:

libuv

Example:

Request
    ↓
Node.js
    ↓
Database Query

While DB is processing:

Node Event Loop
    ↓
Continues serving other requests

Node does not block while waiting for I/O.

======================== What Happens When Nginx Is In Front Of Node ========================

Request flow:

Client
   ↓
Nginx Worker Event Loop
   ↓
Node.js libuv Event Loop
   ↓
Database

Both event loops participate.

Nginx event loop:
handles network layer

Node event loop:
handles application layer

======================== Example Request Lifecycle ========================

Client sends request

Step 1:

Nginx Worker receives connection

Client
   ↓
Nginx Event Loop

Step 2:

Nginx forwards request

Client
   ↓
Nginx
   ↓
Node

Step 3:

Node executes logic

Node
   ↓
DB Query

Step 4:

DB responds

DB
   ↓
Node
   ↓
Nginx
   ↓
Client

======================== Common Misconception ========================

Suppose:

Nginx Worker:
1000 concurrent connections

Node:
100 concurrent requests

Wrong:

1000 × 100
=
100,000 requests

This is incorrect.

======================== Why Capacities Do Not Multiply ========================

Think of a restaurant.

Lobby (Nginx):
1000 people can wait

Kitchen (Node):
100 orders can be cooked

Can restaurant process:

1000 × 100 customers?

No.

Kitchen is bottleneck.

Real system:

1000 requests
    ↓
Nginx
    ↓
100 requests in Node
    ↓
900 waiting

Throughput depends on slowest component.

======================== Real Capacity Formula ========================

System Throughput ≈

min(
Nginx Capacity,
Node Capacity,
Database Capacity
)

Example:

Nginx:
10,000 connections

Node:
2,000 requests

Database:
200 queries

Actual bottleneck:

Database

System behaves closer to:

200 concurrent DB operations

======================== Why Node Handles Many Requests ========================

Node is excellent for:

API servers
webhooks
external API calls
database operations

Because most requests are:

waiting for something

Example:

Request
   ↓
Waiting for DB

During wait:

Event Loop serves other requests

This creates high concurrency.

======================== Where Node Struggles ========================

CPU-intensive work.

Example:

Generate PDF
Video processing
Image resizing
Large calculations

Bad:

for(i=0; i<1e10; i++)
{
   ...
}

Event loop becomes blocked.

All requests wait.

======================== Solving CPU Work In Node ========================

Options:

Worker Threads
Cluster
Separate Worker Services

Example:

HTTP API
    ↓
Queue
    ↓
Worker Process

Keeps API responsive.

======================== What PM2 Cluster Actually Does ========================

Node process uses:

One CPU Core

Server:

8 CPU Cores

Without Cluster:

Core 1 → Used
Core 2 → Idle
Core 3 → Idle
...

With PM2:

pm2 start app.js -i max

Creates:

Node Process 1
Node Process 2
Node Process 3
...
Node Process 8

Now all cores are used.

======================== How Go Is Different ========================

Node:

One Event Loop

Needs:

Cluster
Worker Threads

for CPU scaling.

Go:

Many Goroutines
Few OS Threads

Example:

100,000 Goroutines
        ↓
Go Scheduler
        ↓
8 CPU Cores

Runtime automatically utilizes all cores.

No PM2 needed.

======================== Node vs Go Concurrency ========================

Node:

Request
   ↓
Event Loop
   ↓
Async I/O

Best for:

I/O-bound workloads

Examples:

DB queries
REST APIs
Shopify integrations
Webhooks

Go:

Request
   ↓
Goroutine
   ↓
Scheduler

Excellent for:

I/O workloads
CPU workloads

======================== Modern Production Deployment ========================

Traditional:

Internet
    ↓
Nginx
    ↓
Node.js
    ↓
MySQL

Modern Cloud:

Internet
    ↓
Cloudflare
    ↓
AWS ALB
    ↓
Node.js
    ↓
RDS

Nginx often replaced by:

ALB
Ingress Controllers
Envoy
HAProxy

======================== Key Takeaways ========================

Nginx and Node both have event loops.

Request passes through:

Nginx Event Loop
        ↓
Node libuv Event Loop
        ↓
Database

Concurrency does not multiply.

Capacity is limited by:

slowest layer

Node is extremely good for:

I/O-bound workloads

Node struggles with:

CPU-bound workloads

Go handles CPU concurrency more naturally.

Real bottleneck in most systems is:

Database
External APIs
Network latency

Not Nginx.
Not Node.
```
